1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
Peter: Hello?

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,720
Jan: Oh hello. My name's Jan

3
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:09,350
Are you the right person to talk to about the Buckworth Conservation Group?

4
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:11,710
Peter: Yes, I'm Peter. I'm the secretary.

5
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:16,787
Jan: Good. I've just moved to this area, and I'm interested in getting involved.

6
00:00:16,787 --> 00:00:20,300
I was in a similar group where I used to live

7
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:24,040
Could you tell me something about your activities, please?

8
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:28,082
Peter: Of course. Well, we have a mixture of regular activities and special events.

9
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:32,124
One of the regular ones is trying to keep the beach free of litter.

10
00:00:32,124 --> 00:00:35,880
A few of us spend a couple of hours a month on it

11
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,477
and it's awful how much there is to clear.

12
00:00:38,477 --> 00:00:42,230
I wish people would be more responsible and take it home with them.

13
00:00:42,230 --> 00:00:48,570
Jan: I totally agree. I'd be happy to help with that. Is it OK to take dogs?

14
00:00:48,570 --> 00:00:51,990
Peter: I'm afraid not, as they're banned from the beach itself.

15
00:00:51,990 --> 00:00:55,720
You can take them along the cliffs, though. And children are welcome.

16
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:57,060
Jan: Right.

17
00:00:57,060 --> 00:00:58,900
Peter: We also manage a nature reserve

18
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:02,047
and there's a lot to do there all year round.

19
00:01:02,047 --> 00:01:04,687
For example, because it's a popular place to visit,

20
00:01:04,687 --> 00:01:07,220
we spend a lot of time looking after the paths

21
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:10,260
and making sure they're in good condition for walking.

22
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:12,780
Jan: I could certainly help with that.

23
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:17,416
Peter: Good. And we have a programme of creating new habitats there.

24
00:01:17,416 --> 00:01:22,052
We've just finished making and installing nesting boxes for birds to use,

25
00:01:22,052 --> 00:01:25,280
and next we're going to work on encouraging insects

26
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:28,620
they're important for the biodiversity of the reserve.

27
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:30,200
Jan: They certainly are.

28
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:36,572
Peter: Oh, and we're also running a project to identify the different species of butterflies that visit the reserve.

29
00:01:36,572 --> 00:01:39,590
You might be interested in taking part in that.

30
00:01:39,590 --> 00:01:44,695
Jan: Sure. I was involved in something similar where I used to live,

31
00:01:44,695 --> 00:01:48,230
counting all the species of moths. I'd enjoy that.

32
00:01:48,230 --> 00:01:53,480
Peter: Another job we're doing at the reserve is replacing the wall on the southern side,

33
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:55,580
between the parking area and our woodshed

34
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:58,730
It was badly damaged in a storm last month.

35
00:01:58,730 --> 00:02:00,650
Jan: OK.

36
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:04,040
Peter: Then as I said, we have a programme of events as well

37
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,410
both at the weekend, and during the week.

38
00:02:06,410 --> 00:02:09,520
Jan: Right. I presume you have guided walks?

39
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:15,350
I'd like to get to know the local countryside, as I'm new to the area.

40
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:19,917
Peter: Yes, we do. The next walk is to Ruston Island, a week on Saturday.

41
00:02:19,917 --> 00:02:23,874
We'll be meeting in the car park at Dunsmore Beach at low tide -

42
00:02:23,874 --> 00:02:29,050
that's when the sands are dry enough for us to walk to the island without getting wet.

43
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:30,550
Jan: Sounds good.

44
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:33,199
Peter: The island's a great place to explore.

45
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:36,511
It's quite small, and it's got a range of habitats.

46
00:02:36,511 --> 00:02:40,660
It's also an ideal location for seeing seals just off the coast

47
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:42,140
or even on the beach.

48
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:48,600
Jan: OK. And is there anything we should bring, like a picnic, for instance?

49
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:52,170
Peter: Yes, do bring one, as it's a full-day walk.

50
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:55,520
And of course it'll be wet walking across and back

51
00:02:55,720 --> 00:02:58,240
so make sure your boots are waterproof.

52
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:03,635
Jan: I must buy a new pair - there's a hole in one of my current ones!

53
00:03:03,635 --> 00:03:06,670
Well, I'd definitely like to come on the walk.

54
00:03:06,670 --> 00:03:12,800
Peter: Great. Then later this month we're having a one-day woodwork session in Hopton Wood.

55
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:18,060
Jan: I've never tried that before. Is it OK for beginners to take part?

56
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:21,348
Peter: Definitely. There'll be a couple of experts leading the session,

57
00:03:21,348 --> 00:03:26,430
and we keep the number of participants down, so you'll get as much help as you need.

58
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:30,270
Jan: Excellent! I'd love to be able to make chairs.

59
00:03:30,270 --> 00:03:33,021
Peter: That's probably too ambitious for one day!

60
00:03:33,021 --> 00:03:38,178
You'll be starting with wooden spoons, and of course learning how to use the tools.

61
00:03:38,178 --> 00:03:41,960
And anything you make is yours to take home with you.

62
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:45,360
Jan: That sounds like fun. When is it?

63
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:48,603
Peter: It's on the 17th, from 10 a.

64
00:03:48,603 --> 00:03:53,080
m. until 3. There's a charge of £35, including lunch

65
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:56,710
or £40 if you want to camp in the wood.

66
00:03:56,710 --> 00:03:59,400
Jan: I should think I'll come home the same day

67
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:04,000
Well, I'd certainly like to join the group.
